# A12: a documented voyage, read at a desk

`withAtlasSeaTrialRecord` is an additive, pure-clone correction composed **after** `withPlainLanguage`. The frozen 85-session author packet is unchanged.

## Evidence and historical limit

Royal Museums Greenwich identifies a surviving 1765 pamphlet in which Harrison presents his case after H4’s trials. The game’s hotspot is an attributed paraphrase of that documented account, not a fabricated neutral transcript. The catalogue was inspected; the linked Cambridge viewer could not be opened, so no claim is made to have transcribed the pamphlet. [RMG collection record, BGN/14](https://www.rmg.co.uk/collections/objects/rmgc-object-478825).

The museum’s historical account places H4’s Barbados voyage in 1764 and describes the successful result considered in 1765. It also records disagreement over the award and further testing. The result therefore supports a bounded empirical claim, not universal reliability or an uncontested final victory. [RMG: Harrison’s timekeepers](https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/time/harrisons-clocks-longitude-problem).

## Exact meaning change

Before: `reference-timekeeper + @longitude-sea → sea-tested-clock` described physically testing a clock in a moving exposed setting, despite the room’s shore-based desk artwork. Its downstream recipe treated the new output as another live clock.

After: the same clock/reference-time principle is compared with a **genuine historical voyage report**. `sea-tested-clock` is an evidence-bearing conclusion, **Sea-tested reference time**. The empirical result remains useful: it distinguishes an explained navigation method from evidence that one instrument sustained it under voyage conditions.

The unchanged `sea-tested-clock + local-noon → time-longitude` now uses the trial’s maintained-reference example with the local-noon principle to reconstruct the method. It does not pretend paper supplies a current live reading. The original untested-clock route still explains the method without certifying reliability, and the independent lunar route stays available. The report is thus neither a substitute physical clock nor an empty “wrapped document” chore.

No operand, output, recipe ID, input lifecycle, stock grant, carry policy, session, goal requirement or artwork changes. No date/error arithmetic was added. The source IDs and bibliographic URLs are research metadata; the player’s explanation contains no inline citation apparatus. Only the sea-trial objective’s wording is corrected to an open question. Existing explanation milestone IDs/predicates/art are retained while its title and text follow the corrected output meaning.

## Composition boundary

Call `withAtlasSeaTrialRecord(withPlainLanguage(atlasFocused, 'atlas'))`. Applying the old prose helper afterwards would restore the literal-clock claim; the integration test explicitly checks final visible descriptions, hotspot, recipe and milestone text. Other families and archived scenarios are not opted in. The helper is idempotent.

Verification is focused logical/source testing, not human play or actual controller/native-touch evidence. The parent owns the final aggregate and release seal.
